= Anthropomorphism =

== Overview ==
'''Anthropomorphism''' is the attribution of humanlike traits, intentions, emotions, motives, agency, or mental states to nonhuman entities.

Targets can include animals, natural phenomena, machines, organizations, fictional objects, or abstract systems.

== Mental-state attribution ==
People readily interpret movement, faces, voices, and goal-directed behavior in social terms.

This can lead to attributions such as wanting, knowing, trying, feeling, or deciding.

[[Theory of mind]] mechanisms may contribute, but anthropomorphism is not identical to theory of mind.

== Accuracy ==
Anthropomorphism is not automatically false.

Some nonhuman animals genuinely possess capacities homologous or analogous to human perception, emotion, learning, or social cognition.

The scientific question is whether a specific humanlike attribution is supported by evidence.

== Anthropomorphism and anthropocentrism ==
'''Anthropocentrism''' places humans at the center of interpretation or value.

Anthropomorphism attributes humanlike properties.

The two can overlap but are conceptually distinct.

== Technology ==
People anthropomorphize robots, conversational systems, vehicles, and other interactive technologies.

Humanlike language or behavior can increase perceived agency, trust, responsibility, or emotional attachment even when the underlying system differs fundamentally from a person.

== Scientific caution ==
Avoiding anthropomorphism should not become the opposite error of refusing to recognize well-supported continuity across species.

Comparative cognition uses controlled evidence rather than a blanket rule for or against humanlike descriptions.

== References ==
* Nicholas Epley, Adam Waytz & John T. Cacioppo, “On Seeing Human: A Three-Factor Theory of Anthropomorphism” (2007).
* comparative-cognition and human–robot interaction literature.

